esp32s31: hardware JPEG encoder, MJPEG recorder, and native 800x480

Against the 7.1 port tree, which .gitignore excludes (every linux-*-port/ is;
they are 1.7 GB artefacts regenerated by scripts/port-kernel.sh). Baseline is
the espressif BSP in the linux-esp32-s31 submodule, so this carries both this
work and the 7.1 API adaptations made while porting.

  * JPEG encoder for the codec at 0x20344000, sharing the PPA's 2D-DMA.
    800x480 RGB565 -> baseline 4:2:0 in ~7.2 ms, deterministic. The encoder
    must not be given Huffman tables (those registers are the decoder's); SOS
    must be the last marker; out_reorder_en (OUT_CONF0 bit 16, TX0 only) is
    what turns the raster fetch into MCU order; "CSC disabled" is not the reset
    state, because output_sel defaults to RGB888->RGB565.

  * MJPEG recorder behind DRM ioctls, capturing from the display's commit path
    so an idle desktop costs nothing. 2.4% of the CPU while recording.

  * The output buffer is allocated on first encode rather than at probe: it is
    512 KB of the same CMA pool the framebuffers come from.

  * pclk_khz overrides the panel clock; 60 Hz is the default, measured free
    (CoreMark 950 against 949).

  * Native 800x480 is now the boot default for both the console and the
    desktop, which needed three separate fixes:

    - Only two large buffers may exist at once. The console framebuffer and
      the desktop's used to coexist with the private scanout buffer - three
      ~768 KB allocations from a 4 MB CMA pool - so the third was refused and
      there was no desktop. master_set/master_drop now release the in-kernel
      client and rebuild it, from a work item because master_set runs under
      dev->master_mutex and fbdev unregistration takes console locks.

    - A commit that disables the pipe never called pipe_update(), so its flip
      event was never sent and flip_done waited out its full ten seconds -
      twice, because the next commit blocks on the previous one. Handing the
      panel back took 10.4 s and looked like a hung board; it is now 90 ms.

    - The m2m GDMA damage copy hung the machine. It needs dst_x == 0, which a
      centred render never has, so at 640x384 (+80+48) it had never run at
      all; at native it ran for the first time. The engine was fine - a full
      768,000-byte copy takes 6 ms - but dma_sync_wait() spins on a cookie for
      up to five seconds, and one copy per boot runs long, which is what read
      as a dead machine. The wait is now bounded (gdma_timeout_us, 50 ms) and
      falls back to the PPA, so the cost is a frame rather than the board.

  * The damage copy is done inline, not handed to a kworker. DIRTYFB is a
    synchronous commit, so a client that damages every frame paid for the
    hand-off on the next commit, where copy_sync() blocked in flush_work().
    Measured with dirtybench, median ioctl: single rect 3.66 -> 1.62 ms, two
    corners 3.80 -> 1.71, four scattered 3.99 -> 1.86 - 2.15-2.26x, with the
    tail improving as much as the median. 107 waits over ~400 commits cost
    787 ms, i.e. 7.4 ms per wait just to get a kworker scheduled. The
    deferral's rationale is also dormant: lvdesk draws its pointer in LVGL and
    never uses the DRM cursor plane (cursor_moves reads 0). Kept as
    defer_copy= for a client that does drive the cursor.

  * upd_ns was measuring garbage. The early-out for an unchanged plane is a
    `goto arm_event`, and arm_event ends with `t_enter = ktime_get_ns() -
    t_enter` - so every skipped commit subtracted an uninitialised stack value.
    The counter read 3.08 seconds per commit. GCC did not warn.

  * An identity placement for native render, so place_valid is true and the
    cursor plane, CPU copy path and restore blit are not silently disabled.
